================================================================================
2. THE DATA  —  public domain
================================================================================

Applies to everything under docs/data/ and to the downloaded files in
data/raw/.

The station records come from the Federal Communications Commission's Media
Bureau AM and FM queries. A work prepared by an officer or employee of the
United States Government as part of that person's official duties carries no
copyright under 17 U.S.C. 105, so the underlying records are in the public
domain and nothing here restricts their use.

    Source: https://www.fcc.gov/media/radio/am-query
            https://www.fcc.gov/media/radio/fm-query
    Served from: https://transition.fcc.gov/fcc-bin/amq
                 https://transition.fcc.gov/fcc-bin/fmq

Changes made to the original records
------------------------------------
The files in docs/data/ are modified derivatives, not verbatim copies:

  * Four separate query results (full power FM, low power FM, FM translators,
    AM) combined into one table.
  * Coordinates converted from degrees/minutes/seconds to signed decimal.
  * Several records per facility reduced to one, preferring a licence over a
    permit, and an AM station's separate day and night rows folded into one
    row carrying both powers.
  * Records with a placeholder call sign (NEW, -) and cancelled records
    dropped.
  * A derived "live" column added, distinguishing a station on the air from a
    proposal — which the FCC's own status column does not do consistently
    across borders. See the note in fcc.py.
  * Line endings normalised to LF.

Note that the FCC does not warrant these records, and neither does this
repository. They describe licences, not what is audible, and not what is
playing. See the About page.

Foreign records
---------------
Records for stations in Canada, Mexico and thirty-odd other countries are the
FCC's own, filed under the international coordination agreements. They are US
Government records and sit under the same public domain notice, but they are
second-hand: the authoritative source for a Canadian station is ISED, and for
a Mexican one the IFT.
